## 6.1 HAIR IDENTITY

Use a short, thick, layered hairstyle.

Color:

- Dark chocolate brown.
- Nearly black in deep shadow regions.
- Restrained muted burgundy-brown variation.
- Slightly lighter brown on selected upper surfaces.

The hair should NOT be uniformly pure black.

It should NOT use random colorful highlights.

The hairstyle must be clearly different from Frontend's predominantly black hair and CEO's gray-highlighted senior hairstyle.

## 6.2 HAIR GEOMETRY

Build the hairstyle from intentionally layered voxel clusters.

Recommended semantic groups:

- Hair_Crown_Base
- Hair_Crown_Layers
- Hair_Front_Fringe
- Hair_Left_Temple
- Hair_Right_Temple
- Hair_Side_Layers
- Hair_Back_Volume
- Hair_Back_Taper

Adapt names to the current rig architecture.

The visible silhouette must have:

- A thick stepped upper crown.
- Irregular but controlled block layering.
- A gently asymmetric front hairline.
- Angular front fringe sections.
- Distinct side volume.
- Compact layered rear geometry.
- A well-defined hair-to-face boundary.

Avoid a hairstyle made from one single rectangular cube.

Avoid perfectly flat front edges.

Avoid excessively random individual cubes.

The character should look like a carefully sculpted voxel figure.

## 6.3 FRONT VIEW

From the front:

- Hair crown has readable height.
- Front fringe has multiple stepped layers.
- Fringe frames the upper forehead.
- Hair does not cover the eyes.
- Glasses remain clearly visible.
- Left/right silhouette may have subtle intentional asymmetry.

Do not create a perfectly horizontal solid black hair block.

## 6.4 SIDE VIEWS

From either side:

- Crown has meaningful volume.
- Hair layers overlap naturally.
- Temple geometry is distinct.
- Hair transitions cleanly above the ear.
- Side hair is not an undetailed flat wall.
- Rear hair has a visible stepped continuation.

Avoid clipping through the ear or glasses frame.

## 6.5 BACK VIEW

The rear hairstyle must be deliberately modeled.

It must show:

- Layered voxel structure.
- A thicker upper back section.
- Controlled lower tapering.
- Small variations in block depth.
- Consistent left/right overall mass.

Do not leave the back as one featureless brown cube.

The reference specifically includes rear turnaround views; use them.

## 6.6 HAIR MATERIALS

Use a small controlled palette rather than random materials.

Suggested material relationships:

Base:
Dark chocolate brown.

Shadow:
Very dark brown with a muted reddish undertone.

Midtone:
Warm dark brown.

Highlight:
Slightly lighter muted brown.

Material properties:

- Mostly matte.
- High roughness.
- Low metallic response.
- No glossy wet-hair appearance.
- Crisp geometric edges.

Use geometry and lighting to create volume.

Do not rely on painted dark stripes over a single cubic hair mesh as the only source of layering.

## 6.7 HAIR FLICKER PREVENTION

Inspect:

- Overlapping hair planes.
- Coplanar layer surfaces.
- Hair intersecting ears.
- Hair intersecting glasses.
- Hair intersecting the forehead.
- Duplicate internal surfaces.

Prefer clean geometric construction.

Remove redundant coincident faces.

Use intentional depth variation between layers.

Do not apply a global polygonOffset workaround.

## 6.8 REQUIRED HAIR INSPECTION

Before considering the hairstyle complete, inspect:

- Front.
- Front-right.
- Right.
- Back-right.
- Back.
- Back-left.
- Left.
- Front-left.

Compare the silhouette with the supplied character sheet.

If the front view looks correct but the side or back becomes a flat cube, the hairstyle is NOT complete.

Do not proceed to final visual approval until the hair is coherent in all views.

